.TH RXERANK 1 "Aug 2026" Linux "User Manuals"
.SH NAME
rxerank \- find where a string sits in the set a regular expression describes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rxerank
[\fIoptions\fR]
.I regex
[\fIstring\fR ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rxerank
is the inverse of
.BR rxenum (1).
Where rxenum turns an index into the member of the set that sits at it,
rxerank turns a member back into the index it sits at \(en rank, the inverse
of seek. Given the
.I regex
and one or more strings, it prints, for each, the number rxenum would need to
reach it:
.PP
.RS
.B rxerank '[0-9A-Za-z]{8}' passw0rd
.RE
.PP
The match is anchored and whole: the string has to equal a member exactly, not
merely begin one. A string that is no member of the set prints nothing and
sets the exit status; see EXIT STATUS.
.PP
The index is numbered as
.BR rxenum 's
is: from one by default, from zero under
.BR \-z .
So the number rxerank prints for a string is the one
.B rxenum \-f
takes to print that string back.
.SH DUPLICATES
A set can hold the same member more than once. A class written
.BR [aa] ,
an alternation whose branches overlap such as
.BR "(ab|a)(b|bc)" ,
or two spellings that meet at the empty string as in
.BR "(a|)(|a)" ,
all reach one string by more than one path. rxerank is a bijection at the index
level \(en every index is one member \(en but not at the string level, so it is
many-valued, and the modes below choose what to do about that.
.TP
.B \-a
List every index the string reaches, one per line, in place of just the first.
When more than one string is given each line is tagged with its string, so the
answers stay apart.
.TP
.B \-c
Print how many indices the string reaches rather than the indices themselves.
A count above one is a proof of duplication. The count is exact and costs
nothing to be large: it is tallied without any index being built, so a string
reached four billion ways is counted as fast as one reached once. Run it before
.B \-a
on a set you do not trust, to learn whether the list would be endless.
.TP
.B \-q
Quiet. Print nothing; let the exit status alone report membership. Useful as a
test in a script.
.PP
With no mode flag, rxerank prints one line per string: the least of the indices
that string reaches, which for a member that appears once is simply its index.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-i
Caseless. Parse the regex as
.B rxenum \-i
would, so the set is the same one being ranked into.
.TP
.B \-s
Dot matches a newline too, as
.BR rxenum \-s .
.TP
.B \-z
Number from zero, matching
.BR "rxenum \-z" .
The default is from one.
.PP
With no strings on the command line, rxerank reads them from standard input,
one per line, and answers each in turn \(en so a file of candidates can be
ranked in one pass.
.SH WHAT RANK CAN AND CANNOT DO
rxerank answers every finite set: literals, classes, alternation, concatenation,
subexpressions, fixed or bounded repetition
.RB ( {m,n} ),
the combinatorial choice
.RB ( {{k}} " and " {{k!}} ),
the keyed shuffle
.RB ( (?~key:re) ),
left-to-right ordering
.RB ( (?L) )
and backreferences. A set it cannot rank it refuses by name rather than answer
wrongly, with a line to standard error and exit status 2 \(en which now means
only the infinite sets, anything with
.BR * ", " +
or an open-ended
.BR {n,} .
So a lottery ticket can be ranked into its draw \(en
.B rxerank '( 0[1-9]| [1-5][0-9]| 60){{6}}' ' 04 08 15 16 23 42'
finds where one Mega-Sena game falls among the fifty million.
.SH VERIFYING AGAINST RXENUM
rank and seek are inverses, which is easy to check by hand:
.PP
.RS
.B rxenum \-f 124 '[0-9]{3}'   \(->  123
.br
.B rxerank '[0-9]{3}' 123      \(->  124
.RE
.PP
Over a whole finite set the indices rxerank returns, gathered across every
member, are a permutation of the numbers rxenum would enumerate: each reached
once, none missed, none invented.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every string given was a member.
.TP
.B 1
At least one string was not a member of the set.
.TP
.B 2
The set is one rank cannot handle; standard error names why.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B rxerank '\ed{4}-\ed{2}-\ed{2}' 2024-03-15
Where a given date falls in the space of all such dates.
.TP
.B rxerank -c '(a|a)(b|b)' ab
Prints 4: the four ways the set spells the one string.
.TP
.B rxerank -a '(a|)(|a)' ''
The indices at which the empty string is reached.
.TP
.B rxerank '(\ed{3})-\e1' 507-507
Where a repeated-field code falls; the backreference must echo the group.
.TP
.B cut -f2 accounts.txt | rxerank -q '[a-z]{3,8}' ; echo $?
Test a column of names for membership, silently, and read the verdict from the
exit status.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rxenum (1),
.BR rxedot (1)
